WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.081
If we explore every feasible

2
00:00:02.144 --> 00:00:03.480
path of our toy once

3
00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:05.579
we find six hundred forty paths

4
00:00:05.579 --> 00:00:07.487
ten paths through the header

5
00:00:07.551 --> 00:00:09.395
times sixty-four ways through

6
00:00:09.459 --> 00:00:10.540
the payload loop.

7
00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:12.750
Yet together they cover only

8
00:00:12.817 --> 00:00:15.474
twenty-one branches, because one branch

9
00:00:15.474 --> 00:00:17.268
x four greater than seven

10
00:00:17.268 --> 00:00:18.730
can never be reached.

11
00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:19.948
In the paper

12
00:00:20.015 --> 00:00:22.686
GNU grep two point two has four thousand

13
00:00:22.753 --> 00:00:24.956
one hundred eighty-four branches

14
00:00:24.956 --> 00:00:27.160
and its paths cannot be counted.

15
00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:29.433
The paper's key observation

16
00:00:29.502 --> 00:00:32.043
full branch coverage needs at most as

17
00:00:32.112 --> 00:00:34.310
many runs as there are branches.

18
00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:37.782
So a strategy should hunt for new branches

19
00:00:37.782 --> 00:00:39.380
not visit every path.

20
00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:41.441
To compare strategies

21
00:00:41.441 --> 00:00:44.200
each gets a session of one hundred runs.

22
00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:47.236
A strategy can restart on random inputs

23
00:00:47.236 --> 00:00:49.990
or negate a condition of an earlier run.

24
00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:51.616
Both cost one run

25
00:00:51.687 --> 00:00:54.097
except that an infeasible negation

26
00:00:54.168 --> 00:00:55.940
costs only a solver call.

27
00:00:56.290 --> 00:00:59.104
On seed one, random testing reaches twelve

28
00:00:59.171 --> 00:01:01.048
branches by run twenty-five

29
00:01:01.115 --> 00:01:02.723
and then never gets past

30
00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:04.130
the equality checks.

31
00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:06.807
The classic concolic strategy

32
00:01:06.879 --> 00:01:08.470
is depth-first search.

33
00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:11.412
Its bounded form, with depth twelve

34
00:01:11.412 --> 00:01:13.140
is the task's baseline.

35
00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:14.986
Depth-first search dives

36
00:01:15.049 --> 00:01:16.296
once along the path

37
00:01:16.296 --> 00:01:18.354
flipping each condition in turn

38
00:01:18.416 --> 00:01:20.224
and then always backtracks to

39
00:01:20.286 --> 00:01:21.970
the most recent fork first.

40
00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:25.190
But on every path, the last six forks belong

41
00:01:25.255 --> 00:01:26.560
to the payload loop.

42
00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:28.883
So after one dive through the header

43
00:01:28.937 --> 00:01:31.017
it spends the rest of its budget on the

44
00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:32.830
sixty-four ways through the loop.

45
00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:35.687
On seed one: one hundred runs

46
00:01:35.687 --> 00:01:38.780
six header paths, fourteen branches.

47
00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:41.211
The paper's simplest fix is

48
00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:42.720
random branch search.

49
00:01:43.070 --> 00:01:44.603
Negate a random condition

50
00:01:44.664 --> 00:01:45.829
of the current run

51
00:01:45.890 --> 00:01:47.913
and restart on fresh inputs after

52
00:01:47.975 --> 00:01:49.630
a stretch with nothing new.

53
00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:51.229
It is slow at first

54
00:01:51.291 --> 00:01:53.352
because most conditions on a path

55
00:01:53.414 --> 00:01:55.100
are the six payload checks.

56
00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:58.262
But it does not get stuck: on seed one

57
00:01:58.262 --> 00:02:01.290
eighteen branches after one hundred runs.

58
00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:04.396
CREST's new strategy uses the program's

59
00:02:04.461 --> 00:02:05.691
control-flow graph

60
00:02:05.756 --> 00:02:07.440
which branch can come next

61
00:02:07.504 --> 00:02:08.670
after each branch.

62
00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:09.971
For each branch

63
00:02:10.031 --> 00:02:12.111
the distance is the number of steps

64
00:02:12.171 --> 00:02:14.073
on the shortest static path to a

65
00:02:14.133 --> 00:02:15.500
branch not yet covered.

66
00:02:15.850 --> 00:02:17.394
One breadth-first search

67
00:02:17.459 --> 00:02:18.810
gives every distance.

68
00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:21.291
After one run of all zeros

69
00:02:21.291 --> 00:02:23.343
the run took branch five

70
00:02:23.343 --> 00:02:25.000
x zero is not three.

71
00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:28.610
Its other side, branch four, is uncovered

72
00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:30.550
so its distance is zero.

73
00:02:31.130 --> 00:02:34.377
CFG-directed search negates the condition whose

74
00:02:34.446 --> 00:02:37.210
other side is closest to uncovered code.

75
00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:39.732
It adds to that distance how often

76
00:02:39.796 --> 00:02:41.968
the side was already tried without

77
00:02:42.032 --> 00:02:43.374
finding anything new

78
00:02:43.438 --> 00:02:45.355
so a close but hopeless target

79
00:02:45.418 --> 00:02:46.760
sinks in the ranking.

80
00:02:47.110 --> 00:02:49.202
A run with new coverage becomes

81
00:02:49.269 --> 00:02:50.349
the current run

82
00:02:50.349 --> 00:02:52.710
a run of failures means a restart.

83
00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:56.166
On seed one, it reaches twenty-one branches

84
00:02:56.166 --> 00:02:59.060
every reachable one, by run twenty-five.

85
00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:02.680
Now the race, averaged over twenty seeds.

86
00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:04.513
After one hundred runs

87
00:03:04.578 --> 00:03:06.641
random testing averages thirteen

88
00:03:06.705 --> 00:03:08.253
point six five branches

89
00:03:08.317 --> 00:03:10.252
and bounded depth-first search

90
00:03:10.316 --> 00:03:11.670
thirteen point seven.

91
00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:13.980
Random branch search reaches

92
00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:15.380
sixteen point nine

93
00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:17.550
and CFG-directed search twenty

94
00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:18.740
point five five.

95
00:03:19.090 --> 00:03:21.233
As in the paper's plot for grep

96
00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:23.310
CFG-directed and random branch

97
00:03:23.377 --> 00:03:24.850
search come out ahead.

98
00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:27.587
And CFG-directed search makes fifty-six

99
00:03:27.648 --> 00:03:29.790
point six solver calls per session

100
00:03:29.851 --> 00:03:31.932
against ninety-four point one five

101
00:03:31.993 --> 00:03:33.523
for random branch search

102
00:03:33.584 --> 00:03:35.604
because it skips conditions whose

103
00:03:35.665 --> 00:03:37.440
other side leads nowhere new.

104
00:03:37.790 --> 00:03:38.678
In the task

105
00:03:38.678 --> 00:03:41.710
solver time counts against the strategy.

106
00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:43.890
Pause and predict.

107
00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:45.853
Move the payload loop to the

108
00:03:45.911 --> 00:03:47.120
start of the program.

109
00:03:49.970 --> 00:03:52.690
How does bounded depth-first search do now?

110
00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:55.891
It backtracks to the deepest forks first

111
00:03:55.891 --> 00:03:57.960
and those are now the header's.

112
00:03:58.310 --> 00:03:59.875
After twenty-five runs

113
00:03:59.943 --> 00:04:02.256
its mean jumps from thirteen point

114
00:04:02.324 --> 00:04:03.957
five five to twenty-one

115
00:04:03.957 --> 00:04:05.590
every reachable branch.

116
00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:09.220
Where the paths explode decides how it does.

117
00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:13.980
So: paths explode, but branches do not.

118
00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:16.766
Depth-first search drowns in the loop

119
00:04:16.766 --> 00:04:19.394
random branch search does not get stuck

120
00:04:19.394 --> 00:04:22.021
and CFG-directed search covers the most

121
00:04:22.085 --> 00:04:23.880
with fewer solver calls than

122
00:04:23.944 --> 00:04:25.290
random branch search.

123
00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:28.576
As an exercise, change the last check to x

124
00:04:28.646 --> 00:04:30.184
four greater than six

125
00:04:30.254 --> 00:04:31.932
and see which strategies

126
00:04:32.002 --> 00:04:33.400
find the new branch.

127
00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:35.910
Next: the real task.
